cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "NavFM: evidence-governed reproduction, data-contract validation and simulator admissibility for visual robot navigation"
message: "If you cite this repository before a formal paper release, please state the exact commit used."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Van Laarhoven"
    given-names: "Frank Asante"
    affiliation: "School of Computing, Newcastle University"
repository-code: "https://github.com/FAVL-AI/navfm"
abstract: >-
  A visual-navigation experiment can complete successfully while changing what
  the experiment means. A checkpoint may load without all parameters executing;
  a loss may be finite while optimising the wrong objective; a scene may render
  correctly while missing floor collision; a physics query may return a plausible
  hit while interrogating the wrong object; and a non-empty sample may still be
  too small to support a scene-level claim. NavFM treats these as research
  problems rather than implementation details, establishing a reproducible path
  from published model release to model-ready trajectory data and then to
  physics-valid simulation. Claims are admitted only after their measurement is
  live, causally relevant, reproducible and bound to immutable provenance.
keywords:
  - visual navigation
  - reproducibility
  - embodied AI
  - robot learning
  - Isaac Sim
  - simulator validation
  - measurement validity
  - evidence gating
license: MIT
